import re
def clean(t):
 t=str(t)
 for word in ['at','and','all','All','be','are','from','to','into','through','between','with','most','cost','costs','distance','base','by','default','district','east','west','north','south','North','South','East','West','vertical','horizontal','is','maximum','mod','modulo','offset','on','position','tile','changes','Finite','currently','November','December','Off']:
  t=re.sub(r'\b('+word+r')(?=\d)',r'\1 ',t)
 t=t.replace('a844','an 844').replace('an844','an 844').replace('board;18','board; 18').replace('bound:4','bound: 4').replace('actions:4','actions: 4').replace('deadline:1','deadline: 1').replace('conversion:2','conversion: 2')
 return t
def p(t):d.add_paragraph(clean(t))
def h(t,l=1):d.add_heading(clean(t),l)
def page(t):d.add_page_break();h(t)
def bullet(t):d.add_paragraph(clean(t),style='List Bullet')
def table(head,rows):
 t=d.add_table(rows=1, cols=len(head));t.style='Light Shading Accent 1'
 for c,v in zip(t.rows[0].cells,head):c.text=clean(v)
 for row in rows:
  for c,v in zip(t.add_row().cells,row):c.text=clean(v)

d.add_paragraph('LITTLE HARBOR POST / GAME OFF 2026',style='Subtitle');d.add_heading('Night Shift Atlas',0);d.add_paragraph('The streets move. The letters must arrive.',style='Subtitle');d.add_picture(str(root/'media/cover.png'),width=Inches(6));p('Project guide, user manual and interview preparation');p('Version 1.0 • 14 November 2026 • Prepared for M J Anthony');p('AI-assisted project. This document explains the implementation; it does not claim that M personally wrote, tested or already understands every part of it. Study the source and rehearse the routes before discussing them as personal technical experience.')
page('1. The project in plain words');p('Night Shift Atlas is a small browser puzzle game. A courier walks along connected railway tracks to collect letters, then returns to a depot. The player can slide whole rows of the map. Sliding moves the tracks, letters, depot and courier together, so the map itself becomes the puzzle.');h('The one-line pitch',2);p('A quiet postal puzzle where you shift the city to find a way home.');h('Input → workflow → output',2);p('Input: a walk direction or a district shift. Workflow: validate that action, copy the current state, change row offsets or position, collect a letter if reached, then redraw the map. Output: a new board arrangement, updated action count and eventually a completed dispatch.');h('What makes it a game rather than a visual toy',2);bullet('A finite 18-route campaign with progression in letter count and fixed-district constraints.');bullet('A precise movement rule, a measurable objective, shortest-route targets, completion records and a final ending.');bullet('Undo, hints and a workshop support experimentation instead of merely displaying a scene.');h('Honest interview framing',2);p('Suggested framing: “This was an AI-assisted project under my direction. The main mechanic is a shifting grid. The important technical choices are a pure transition function, a compact finite state and breadth-first search for guaranteed shortest hints.” Only describe implementation details you can actually explain. Do not say you personally wrote or manually tested code that you did not.');
page('2. Start playing');h('Hosted play',2);p('The intended delivery is the itch.io browser page. Click Launch game, read the short instructions and begin. On phones, use Focus map so the board and touch controls stay together. No game account, install, payment or API key is required. The project publishing account is separate from player access.');h('Offline play',2);p('The release/index.html file contains the complete game, styles, campaign and solver worker source. Open it in a modern browser. Local-file storage behavior varies by browser, so use Export progress if saves matter. The development source uses native modules and is best served through a local HTTP server.');h('The objective',2);p('Collect every green envelope in any order, then reach the red house-shaped depot. Both ends of a track seam must connect. Shifting wraps a district left or right; walking does not wrap. An envelope is collected by occupying its tile, not by shifting an entire row that happens to contain it.');h('Controls',2);table(['Action','Keyboard','Touch / mouse'],[['Walk','Arrows or WASD','Compass pad or adjacent tile'],['Select row','1, 2, 3, 4','Tap a non-adjacent tile in the row'],['Shift row','Q left; E or Shift right','Brass arrows at either end'],['Undo','Z','Undo'],['Reset','R','Reset'],['Hint','Button only','A gentle hint'],['Close dialog','Escape','Close ×']]);p('Keyboard game shortcuts are ignored while a dialog or input is active. This prevents typing a route name from moving the courier.');
page('3. Your first dispatch, step by step');p('The first map is a deliberate teaching route. A single horizontal shift aligns the vertical track in district 2 with the courier in district 1.');d.add_picture(str(root/'media/desktop.png'),width=Inches(5.65));table(['Step','Action','Result'],[['1','Select district 2; shift it left once','The first north-south seam connects.'],['2','Walk south','Enter the aligned vertical track.'],['3','Walk south','Collect the green envelope.'],['4','Walk east','Follow the lower horizontal track.'],['5','Walk east','Reach the depot and complete the route.']]);p('The verified shortest route is five actions. The first shift counts as one action; collecting a letter is a consequence of walking, not a separate action.');
page('4. The atlas and campaign');p('All routes are open from the atlas. There are three chapters of six dispatches each. A check indicates completion. A star indicates a solver-par route completed without hints. The saved record keeps the lowest action count, with an unhinted tie preferred.');
levels=json.loads(subprocess.check_output(['node','--input-type=module','-e',"import{LEVELS}from'/downloads/night-shift-atlas/src/levels.mjs';console.log(JSON.stringify(LEVELS))"]))
table(['No.','Dispatch','Letters','Par','Fixed rows'],[[l['id'],l['name'],len(l['stamps']),l['par'],','.join(str(x+1) for x in l['locked']) or 'None'] for l in levels]);p('Par is computed by breadth-first search with every walk and shift costing one. It is a mathematical shortest-action result for these exact rules, not a claim about how long a person should take.');
page('5. Undo, reset, hints and records');h('Undo',2);p('Before a successful action, the app pushes a copy of state and move count onto history. Undo restores that snapshot. Invalid actions do not add history or increment moves. History is session-local and is not stored across reloads. There is no designed cap on the current-session history.');h('Reset',2);p('Reset rebuilds the initial state for the current level, clears its action history, moves and hint count, and leaves earlier completion records intact. It does not erase the entire campaign.');h('A gentle hint',2);p('The app sends the current map and state to a dedicated worker. The worker runs BFS and returns a route. The UI displays only the first action plus how many shortest-route actions remain. It never automatically plays the route. Request IDs discard late results after another action or level change. A synchronous fallback is used if worker creation fails.');h('Unsolvable custom states',2);p('A custom map can have no valid route. The editor rejects such a map before playtest or export. The solver reports no path or a search limit honestly. The campaign contains verified route witnesses. A solvability proof is not a claim that the game is enjoyable or accessible to everyone.');h('Completion',2);p('All mail bits must be set and the courier must occupy the depot tile. Finishing opens a delivery dialog with actions, par and hints used. The eighteenth route has a final harbor-at-dawn ending. Replays remain available.');
page('6. Phone play and accessibility');d.add_picture(str(root/'media/mobile-focus-390.png'),width=Inches(1.95));p('Focus map hides the page introduction and side text on small screens, preserving the map, shift buttons, compass pad and toolbar. Full page returns to the editorial layout. Actual touch-route checks were performed at320px and390px widths in a844px-high browser viewport. This does not certify every physical phone or browser.');h('Available options',2);bullet('Higher contrast changes the paper, ink, muted text, boundaries and red accent. Mail remains white on dark green; depot and courier have different shapes.');bullet('Reduced motion respects the operating-system preference initially and can be changed locally. The game has no flashing or timed hazards.');bullet('Sound effects are optional and off by default. No sound is needed to understand a route.');bullet('Controls have labels; dialogs support Escape and keyboard focus. Keyboard shortcuts do not steal text entry.');h('Limits',2);p('This is still a spatial game. Screen-reader labels are implemented, but no screen-reader-user study or specialist audit was completed. Some row-edge arrows are narrow on phones; the larger selected-district shift buttons and compass pad are the main accessible touch route.');
page('7. Saves, backups and privacy');p('The single localStorage key is night-shift-atlas-v1. The saved object contains a version, settings, completion records, selected campaign index, a seen-instructions flag and the current campaign state. It does not contain names, emails, passwords or analytics identifiers.');table(['Field','Meaning'],[['v','Backup schema version, currently1'],['prefs','Sound, motion and contrast choices'],['records','Best actions and hinted flag keyed by level ID'],['index','Current campaign level, zero-based'],['run','Map index, current state, move count and hints'],['seen','Whether first-run instructions were dismissed']]);h('Backup workflow',2);p('Settings → Export progress downloads JSON. On another device, Settings → Import backup reads the file. The importer validates schema version, records and index before using it. Device settings are kept from the importing browser. The map-state check validates dimensions and ranges, not cryptographic authenticity. A person can edit their own save; this is not a competitive leaderboard.');h('Failure behavior',2);p('If storage is unavailable, the footer reports that a backup should be exported. A malformed save is ignored. Private browsing, storage eviction, iframe partitioning, clearing site data and moving between origins may lose or separate progress. There is no cloud sync. Workshop drafts are not persisted, so export the map code to keep them.');
page('8. The mapmaker workshop');p('The workshop starts from a solvable sample. Choose Tracks, Courier, Depot, Letters or Fix district, then tap a tile. Tracks cycle through twelve masks, including empty, straight, corners, three-way junctions and a four-way junction.');table(['Tool','Effect'],[['Tracks','Cycle shape at the selected original tile'],['Courier','Set the starting tile'],['Depot','Set the final destination tile'],['Letters','Toggle a stop; maximum three'],['Fix district','Toggle whether that whole row can shift']]);h('Validation',2);p('The map must be4×4 with16 integer masks between0 and15. Start and depot must be distinct valid track tiles. One to three unique mail stops must lie on track and differ from start and depot. Fixed rows must be integers0 through3. The solver must find a delivery route before playtest or export.');h('Sharing',2);p('Export map code validates the map, computes its par, fills a JSON text area and downloads an atlas-map.json file. Another player can paste that code into the import area. The importer limits text length, picks allowed fields and treats names as text. It does not run imported code or upload the map to a server.');h('Why there is no online workshop feed',2);p('A feed would need identity, a backend, abuse controls and hosting. Manual code sharing preserves a fully free, static product without creating an unapproved service dependency.');
page('9. Architecture and source map');table(['File','Responsibility'],[['index.html','Semantic page layout and dialog container'],['src/engine.mjs','Pure transition rules, validation, BFS and state encoding'],['src/levels.mjs','Fixed campaign data, par values and route witnesses'],['src/solver-worker.mjs','Worker message adapter around the solver'],['src/app.mjs','Rendering, controls, saves, sound, workshop and dialog flow'],['src/style.css','Paper-map visual system and responsive layouts'],['scripts/generate.mjs','Reproducible seeded campaign generator'],['scripts/build.mjs','Self-contained HTML build including Blob worker source'],['tests/engine.test.mjs','Node rule and solver tests'],['scripts/*test.mjs','Browser UI/release regression checks'],['release/index.html','Published playable artifact']]);h('Separation of concerns',2);p('The engine never imports the DOM, storage or audio. This makes it testable in Node and reusable inside a worker. The UI does not reimplement movement legality. Both hints and gameplay call the same step function, reducing rule drift. The build embeds those same sources instead of writing a second release-only engine.');h('Runtime dependencies',2);p('Only browser features: JavaScript modules, SVG, DOM events, dialogs, Blob URLs, workers, localStorage and Web Audio. No game library, CDN, backend or network fetch is required to play. Automated browser testing is a development-only dependency.');
page('10. The state model');p('A tile has an immutable identity from0 to15. Its mask and special role are attached to that identity. A district offset tells us which tile identity is visible at each board coordinate. This avoids physically copying or rotating arrays on every shift.');h('State tuple',2);p('State = {o, p, m}. o is a four-element row-offset array. p is the courier’s current board cell. m is the collected-letter bitmask. Offsets are0 to3, board position0 to15, and mail bits depend on how many stops the map has.');h('Finding the tile under a coordinate',2);p('Let r=floor(p/4), c=p mod4. Original tile index =4r + mod(c-o[r],4). The normalized modulo function is ((a%n)+n)%n so negative shifts are handled correctly in JavaScript.');h('Shifting',2);p('A shift increments the chosen row offset modulo4. If the courier is in that row, its column also increments by the same signed amount modulo4. Other rows do not move the courier. Mail stops and depot automatically move because their roles are tied to tile identity.');h('Walking',2);p('Walking first checks board bounds, then checks the current tile has an outgoing direction bit and the adjacent tile has the matching incoming bit. Both must be true. The courier moves exactly one board cell. After either valid action, collect checks the tile under the courier.');
page('11. Track masks and bit operations');table(['Direction','Bit value','Opposite'],[['North','1','South4'],['East','2','West8'],['South','4','North1'],['West','8','East2']]);p('A track mask combines open directions with bitwise OR. Vertical is1|4=5. Horizontal is2|8=10. A southeast corner is2|4=6. All directions is15. Empty is0. A test such as mask&2 asks whether east is open.');h('Mail mask',2);p('If a map has three stops, the collected states range from000 to111 in binary. Reaching stop i sets m |=1<<i. The target full mask is(1<<stopCount)-1. Collecting the same letter twice cannot increase the count because OR is idempotent.');h('Worked transition',2);p('On the first dispatch, district2 starts with offset1. Shifting it left changes1 to0, aligning its first vertical tile with the courier’s column. The courier remains in district1 until walking south. Reaching original tile8 sets the first mail bit from0 to1. The final east step reaches depot tile10, so the win predicate becomes true.');h('Common mistakes',2);bullet('Using JavaScript remainder directly for negative offsets produces negative columns.');bullet('Moving the courier on every row shift would violate the physical model.');bullet('Checking only one track endpoint allows walking through a wall.');bullet('Treating special tiles as fixed board coordinates breaks shifting semantics.');
page('12. Solver and level generation');h('Why BFS',2);p('Every legal action has equal cost1. Breadth-first search visits states in nondecreasing distance from the start. The first winning state removed from the queue therefore has a shortest action path. Weighted A* or Dijkstra would add complexity without a benefit for these rules.');h('State-space bound',2);p('There are4^4 row-offset arrangements,16 courier positions and2^k collected-mail states. For k=3 this is256×16×8=32,768 keys. Some combinations are unreachable. Fixed rows reduce reachable arrangements. The default100,000 visit limit exceeds the full legal key space of supported maps.');h('Storage',2);p('A dense integer key packs the offsets, courier position and bitmask. An Int32Array stores each predecessor, initialized to-1. An Int16Array stores the action chosen. A queue holds states. The root points to itself. When a win is found, follow predecessors back to the root and reverse the action list.');h('Generation',2);p('A fixed linear-congruential seed selects tile masks and distinct special tiles. Candidate maps are solved. Filters require a minimum number of shifts and walking actions and a route-length band that generally grows later in the campaign. The first map is authored separately as a teaching board. Resulting maps and shortest paths are committed as fixed data; the game never generates a random campaign at runtime.');h('Limits of the method',2);p('Shortest route length is only a proxy for difficulty. The later route par values are not strictly monotonic. Solver verification proves a solution exists; independent human playtesting is still needed to measure clarity, delight and perceived challenge.');
page('13. Design decisions and reasons');decisions=[('Native browser stack','Small static game, fast loading and no install or paid hosting dependency.'),('Finite4×4 board','Keeps the spatial puzzle readable and makes exhaustive shortest-path search practical.'),('Offset model rather than shuffling arrays','Preserves tile identity and makes shifts constant-time state changes.'),('Uniform action costs','Lets the player understand par and permits plain BFS.'),('Pure immutable transitions','One source of rules for gameplay, hints and tests; undo snapshots are predictable.'),('Worker solver with fallback','Keeps search away from rendering, while remaining playable where workers fail.'),('Optional hints, no autoplay','Supports learning without removing the player’s decision.'),('No timer or lives','Encourages experimentation and reduces pressure; mistakes are reversible.'),('All campaign routes open','Players can skip a hard route; completion records still show progress.'),('Local saves and manual sharing','Avoids accounts, tracking, backend cost and moderation burdens.'),('System fonts and original procedural assets','No license-sensitive downloads, network delay or runtime asset failures.'),('Phone Focus map','Keeps controls and board visible together rather than merely shrinking a desktop layout.'),('Single-file release','Reliable upload, offline play and no relative-path or CDN failures.'),('Explicit AI disclosure','Prevents false personal-authorship claims and meets the event/platform requirements.')]
for i,(title,reason) in enumerate(decisions,1):h(f'{i}. {title}',2);p(reason)
page('14. Verification and release process');p('The test evidence describes what was actually checked in this build. It is not an independent audit, device-lab certificate or human-playtest report.');table(['Layer','Checked'],[['Engine','30 Node tests: all18 par routes, immutability, wrap rules, endpoint matching, fixed rows, collection, win conditions, invalid imports, keys, unsolvable maps and search limits'],['Browser UI','All18 campaign routes completed through controls in par; hints, undo, reload persistence, backup export, settings and workshop checks'],['Release','The same browser suite against the self-contained HTML, not only development modules'],['Responsive','No horizontal overflow at320,390,768 and1440px'],['Touch','First dispatch completed with actual touch actions at320/390px, with board and compass visible together'],['Visual','Desktop, phone Focus map, high contrast and demo frames inspected'],['Demo','Actual release-build gameplay, AI narration, readable captions; local recording labeled']]);h('A caught release bug',2);p('An early build used a replacement string that interpreted JavaScript dollar patterns, collapsing the $$ selector helper into $. The release test failed immediately with a duplicate declaration. The builder was changed to use a replacement callback, then the full release suite was rerun successfully. This is why testing the artifact matters, not only its source.');h('Release checklist',2);bullet('Run npm test; rebuild release; run release browser suite.');bullet('Check first-route touch play and inspect phone/desktop screenshots.');bullet('Upload the exact release, verify live start and completion, and confirm no-payments mode.');bullet('Publish public source and submit the playable project to the jam. A project page alone is not a jam entry.');
page('15. Security, reliability and honest limits');h('Untrusted input',2);p('Workshop imports are JSON data. The importer takes known fields and validates ranges. Route names are inserted using textContent or HTML escaping. A browser test used a name containing an image/onerror string and confirmed it remained plain text. Imports do not execute scripts.');h('Save integrity',2);p('Local progress is not signed. This is acceptable because there is no competitive shared leaderboard or financial reward inside the game. Range validation prevents malformed state from being used accidentally; it does not prevent a player from forging their own records.');h('Reliability tradeoffs',2);p('The UI redraws all16 tiles after each action. This is simple and adequate at this scale, but it is not an efficient pattern for thousands of animated tiles. The synchronous hint fallback could briefly block on slower devices, although the search space is bounded. There is no service worker or installable PWA. The self-contained release can be saved locally for offline play.');h('Known limitations',2);bullet('Finite campaign; no human difficulty study, external playtest panel or formal accessibility audit.');bullet('No background music, multiplayer, cloud saves or online level feed.');bullet('Workshop draft and undo history are session-local; export to preserve a workshop map.');bullet('Browser support is modern-browser based; no physical iPhone/Safari certification was performed.');bullet('Competition submission status must be verified separately from build status.');
page('16. Interview questions: fundamentals');qas=[('What is the core idea?','The player moves through connected tracks while shifting complete map rows. The map arrangement is part of the state and changes the available walking graph.'),('What did you build versus use?','The game uses native browser APIs, original code-drawn assets and a pure JavaScript rules engine. AI assistance was extensive. No third-party runtime game framework is required.'),('Why is this theme-related?','SHIFT is literal row motion and a change in perspective: instead of searching only for a path, the player changes the city to create one.'),('What is the game state?','Four row offsets, courier board position and collected-letter bitmask. Map tile identities, masks and stop roles are fixed level data.'),('Why keep tile identity separate from position?','Special roles travel with tiles during shifts. Identity-based roles avoid manually updating every stop coordinate.'),('How do you check a legal walk?','Check bounds, outgoing direction bit on the current tile, and opposite incoming bit on the destination tile.'),('Why does walking not wrap?','The designed rule distinguishes stepping off the city from the mechanical conveyor-like row shift. The UI explains this and tests enforce it.'),('How does undo work?','Each valid action stores a copied state and move count. Undo pops and restores that snapshot. Invalid actions do not add history.'),('How are letters represented?','Each stop has one bit. Visiting it ORs the bit into the collected mask. The win condition compares against the full mask.'),('How do you prevent rendering injection?','Use textContent for visible names and escape special characters in HTML templates. Imported map JSON is validated and selected fields are copied.')]
for i,(q,a) in enumerate(qas,1):h(f'{i}. {q}',2);p(a)
page('17. Interview questions: algorithms');qas2=[('Why BFS rather than A*?','All actions have equal cost and the state space is at most32,768 supported keys. BFS is simple, complete within this finite graph and shortest by action count.'),('Prove that a returned path is shortest.','BFS expands nodes in layers of distance0,1,2 and so on. With unit edges, any shorter win would have been expanded before the first winning state. Predecessors reconstruct that route.'),('What is the complexity?','O(V+E) time and O(V) memory. Each state has at most12 actions:4 walks and8 row shifts before fixed-row reductions. V is bounded by4^4×16×2^k.'),('What does the integer key do?','It packs row offsets in base4, then appends position in base16 and mail bits in base2^k. Tests check all32,768 keys for collision on a three-stop map.'),('What if shifts had different costs?','BFS would no longer minimize cost. Dijkstra would handle nonnegative unequal weights; A* could help with an admissible heuristic.'),('How are levels guaranteed solvable?','Every campaign map is solved during generation and stores a shortest witness route. Tests replay each witness using the same rules and verify the final win.'),('Does solvable mean fun?','No. Reachability is a correctness property. Fun, teaching quality and perceived difficulty require human playtesting.'),('Why use typed arrays?','Dense bounded keys make fixed-size predecessor arrays simple and memory predictable. A Map would work but adds hashing/object overhead.'),('What prevents stale hints?','Each action or level change increments a request ID. A result is applied only when its ID still matches the current request.'),('What happens if a worker fails?','The UI can run the same solver synchronously. That preserves functionality but can briefly affect responsiveness on slower devices.')]
for i,(q,a) in enumerate(qas2,11):h(f'{i}. {q}',2);p(a)
page('18. Interview questions: product and engineering');qas3=[('Why not React?','This is a small board and local UI, with no backend data layer. Native modules keep deployment and dependencies small. A larger editor or online platform might justify a framework.'),('How do saves work?','A versioned JSON object goes to localStorage. Backup files allow manual device transfer. There is no server, account identity or cloud synchronization.'),('Is it accessible?','It has keyboard and touch controls, labeled tiles, shape cues, high contrast, reduced motion and optional sound. It remains spatial and has not been validated by screen-reader users, so I would not claim full accessibility.'),('How was mobile tested?','Responsive overflow checks at four widths plus actual touch first-route completion at320/390px. Focus map kept board and compass together in an844px viewport. This is not a physical-device lab result.'),('Why a single-file release?','It removes missing-asset paths, CDN failures and install steps. The build embeds the same engine and campaign as the source, and the artifact is tested separately.'),('What was a real bug you caught?','The first bundler replacement treated dollar signs in source as replacement patterns. Release tests caught a duplicate selector declaration. A callback replacement fixed it.'),('What would you improve next?','Human playtesting, better difficulty calibration, stronger keyboard focus preservation during tile redraw, broader browser/device tests and specialist nonvisual design evaluation.'),('How do you keep this free?','Static browser hosting, no runtime AI or API calls, procedural local audio, original code assets and no backend. No payments or donations are enabled for the project.'),('How would online level sharing change the architecture?','It would need a backend, content validation, rate limiting, abuse reports, identity decisions and storage. The current manual JSON sharing avoids that scope.'),('How should AI assistance be described?','Say it was used extensively for development, tests, design and documentation. Explain the parts you understand. Do not imply personal manual authorship or testing that did not occur.')]
for i,(q,a) in enumerate(qas3,21):h(f'{i}. {q}',2);p(a)
page('19. Glossary and quick revision');table(['Term','Meaning here'],[['State','The minimal information needed to continue a route'],['Transition','A legal action producing a new state'],['Graph','All reachable states connected by legal actions'],['BFS','Breadth-first search, a shortest-path algorithm for unit edges'],['Bitmask','Integer whose individual bits represent yes/no flags'],['Modulo','Wrap a column or row offset into0 through3'],['Invariant','A property that should remain true after every action'],['Worker','Browser background execution context for the solver'],['Typed array','Dense numeric array used for predecessors and actions'],['Par','Shortest number of actions under this exact rule set'],['Witness route','A concrete action sequence proving solvability'],['Serialization','Turn state into JSON for storage or sharing'],['Escaping','Treat special text as data rather than HTML markup'],['Procedural audio','Sound synthesized by code rather than a recorded asset']]);h('Five facts to remember',2);bullet('4×4 board;18 fixed campaign routes; maximum3 mail stops.');bullet('State-space bound:4^4×16×2^3=32,768.');bullet('North1, east2, south4, west8; vertical5, horizontal10.');bullet('BFS is shortest because every action costs1.');bullet('Local saves are convenient, not permanent or tamper-proof.');h('A two-minute interview walkthrough',2);p('Start with the plain pitch. Demonstrate the first five-action route. Show one fixed-row/two-letter map. Explain the state tuple and one track-bit check. Describe BFS and its bound. Show a test result, then name one caught release bug and one honest limitation. Finish with the AI disclosure rather than letting an interviewer infer unsupported authorship.');
